CBS News political contributor Bob  Schieffer has covered the bush family's  political fortunes and private  contributions to American life for more  than 40 years he spoke to mrs. Bush in  1988 her first interview after her  husband announced he would run for  president  Bob is with us from Washington Bob so  good to have you with us this morning  you think about Barbara Bush and one of  the things that comes to mind is her  kids calling her the enforcer and she  really was I mean any one of them would  tell you that you know their father they  respected him greatly and they were  always worried about disappointing him  but they were scared of her and I can  tell you that for a fact I mean they  loved her to death but she was the one  who made the rules in the family and she  was the one who enforced them and I'm  telling you she could stare down a  grizzly bear but at the same time she  loved children and she'd love to be  around them when visitors used to come  to Kennebunkport in the summertime she  would sometimes sit a 10 or 12 year old  child next to the President of the  United States and and she was interested  in all children and especially her  children and her grandchildren Bob what  struck you about that 1988 interview  when you interviewed her when her  husband was running for president well  she she was she was you know she knew  who she was and she was very comfortable  with herself and she was not afraid to  say out loud what other people might be  thinking I mean there had been a big  story going around Newsweek and run a  cover that George Bush was a wimp and it  said overcoming the wimp factor and I  asked her about it in that interview and  she said he's not a wimp but if he is we  need a lot of wimps because he is one  tough wimp  Bob she may have been born in New York  but us Texans like to gladly claim her  as one of our own talk about the role  she played as a confidante and advisor  to her husband well she was a confidante  who gave her advice to the president she  was not going on television and and  spelling out what he ought to do or what  he ought not to do but what I always  remember her for is is I mean she didn't  suffer fools gladly and she at some  points could be very off-putting to  people if she didn't agree with them but  she was the mom she was the one in  charge of that family and you know this  long life that they had one of the  things that together I think a lot of  people don't know about when George Bush  George HW Bush her husband was playing  baseball at Yale she loved baseball and  she used to keep all of his she she kept  score and she had all of his the box  scores of all the games he played at  Yale she taught George W Bush how to  keep score in baseball and then when the  grandkids came along she she she taught  them how to keep scores she still had  and I'm supposed somewhere in her and  her property there she still has all of  the box scores of george w bush's  baseball games when he played in Little  League  she was really a fan right up until they  absolutely love love hearing those  stories Bob you know in the final final  days of her life we learned that while  she was battling heart failure she did  also enjoy her favorite bourbon  what does that tell us about Barbara  Bush and I don't think she'd be ashamed  for you to know that because that's  that's the kind of person she was she  was who she was and as I say she was  quite a formidable person all right bob  thanks may we all have a bourbon at hand  after a life like that thanks the flag  at the White House is at half-staff this  morning in memory of Barbara Bush  President Trump  ordered flags to be lowered at all  government buildings and military  outposts president and first lady said  in a statement that they joined the  nation in celebrating the life of an  advocate of the American family
 

